WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.203 --> 00:00:03.620
(atmospheric rock music)

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.270
- These organisms are
beautiful and iconic.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:11.620
There are certain types that are present

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.340
nowhere else on earth.

00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.183
So it's very important to study them.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:24.340
My name is Drew Lohrer,

00:00:24.340 --> 00:00:29.050
and I'm the principal investigator
on Science Under the Ice.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:32.500
Science Under the Ice is what
we've been calling our project

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.120
where we've been studying
the resilience of organisms,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.440
the sea floor organisms,
to climate-related changes.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:42.300
And we do science under
the ice, literally,

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:46.863
by scuba diving under
frozen ocean in Antarctica.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:52.300
The most recent trip was to
Explorers Cove on New Harbor,

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:55.113
which is in the Ross Sea, Antarctica.

00:00:56.750 --> 00:01:00.522
Our team was nine
scientists and technicians

00:01:00.522 --> 00:01:04.750
and, of those, seven of
them were scientific divers

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:06.463
that dived underneath the ice.

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:11.590
We are about to commence
our dive operations

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:14.323
for the morning/afternoon.

00:01:15.194 --> 00:01:19.270
On this trip, we went
diving underneath the ice

00:01:19.270 --> 00:01:22.410
to deploy a large-scale experiment

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.110
involving incubation chambers

00:01:24.110 --> 00:01:26.090
that we deployed to the sea floor.

00:01:26.090 --> 00:01:30.600
We also surveyed the fauna
using standard survey techniques

00:01:30.600 --> 00:01:33.620
and we collected organisms
for isotopic analysis,

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.430
so we can reconstruct food webs

00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:39.400
and how they've changed
since our previous trips.

00:01:39.400 --> 00:01:42.230
The water is -2 degrees Celsius.

00:01:42.230 --> 00:01:43.720
It's very, very cold.

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:47.060
And we have to dive
through ten feet of sea ice

00:01:47.060 --> 00:01:49.270
in order to access our study sites.

00:01:49.270 --> 00:01:52.400
Diving under the ice is quite surreal.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.581
It's like diving in twilight
because the light is dim.

00:01:55.581 --> 00:01:59.320
It's almost in some ways
like diving in outer space

00:01:59.320 --> 00:02:01.100
because of the vistas that you get

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:02.560
towards the underside of the ice

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:04.810
with the light coming through.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.030
And the clarity of the
water is, is unbelievable.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:10.400
It's almost like you're floating in air

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:11.843
rather than floating in water.

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:19.750
I think documenting what is down there now

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:22.780
and how it's changing over
time is really important.

00:02:22.780 --> 00:02:25.784
This year all of us were
struck by just how quickly

00:02:25.784 --> 00:02:29.140
things can change and it shows just how,

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.180
there is essentially climate models

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:34.223
that are predicting changes
in sea ice conditions.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.990
Science Under the Ice is pretty extreme.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:40.870
It's hard to keep the heat up in the huts

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:45.723
because the Sigma stoves
are not working that well.

00:02:45.723 --> 00:02:49.240
That being said, the divers
love diving under the ice

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:52.770
because of the beauty
and the unique experience

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:53.620
that it provides.

